rig":"10-6","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":621.5566813151041,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":321.6666666666667,"r":331.6666666666667,"t":628.2233479817709},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/29","text":"10-6"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10-1","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":614.8900146484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":328.3333333333333,"r":338.3333333333333,"t":621.5566813151041},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/30","text":"10-1"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"100","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":614.5566813151041,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":496.0,"r":506.6666666666667,"t":623.2233479817709},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/31","text":"100"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10 2","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":698.7519434609799,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":320.52859547920895,"r":332.47140452079105,"t":703.0280858358951},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/32","text":"10 2"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Fig. 4: The multilinear rank ( r 1 , r 2 , r 3 ) and average of the multilinear rank ( r 1 + r 2 + r 3 ) / 3 of the solution to (66) with initial condition (67) using backward Euler (left) , DIRK2 (middle) and DIRK3 (right) .","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":408.098904525671,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.00000000000006,"r":523.27741176,"t":428.76047044843756},"charspan":[0,225],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/33","text":"Fig. 4: The multilinear rank ( r 1 , r 2 , r 3 ) and average of the multilinear rank ( r 1 + r 2 + r 3 ) / 3 of the solution to (66) with initial condition (67) using backward Euler (left) , DIRK2 (middle) and DIRK3 (right) ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"As seen in Figure 3, the expected accuracies are observed for the RAIL scheme when using IMEX111, IMEX222 and IMEX443. We used a mesh size N = 80, tolerance ε = 10 -6 , final time T f = 0 . 3, and λ ranging from 0.1 to 1. Despite observing the expected accuracy, the L 1 error for the first-order scheme (and even the second-order scheme) is quite large. However, recall that we do not scale the L 1 error by the measure of the domain, which in this case would be | Ω | = (2 π ) 3 ; scaling by the measure of the domain would provide a better comparison against the L ∞ error which is not as large.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":314.832904525671,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":523.2757306999999,"t":383.31547044843757},"charspan":[0,598],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/34","text":"As seen in Figure 3, the expected accuracies are observed for the RAIL scheme when using IMEX111, IMEX222 and IMEX443. We used a mesh size N = 80, tolerance ε = 10 -6 , final time T f = 0 . 3, and λ ranging from 0.1 to 1. Despite observing the expected accuracy, the L 1 error for the first-order scheme (and even the second-order scheme) is quite large. However, recall that we do not scale the L 1 error by the measure of the domain, which in this case would be | Ω | = (2 π ) 3 ; scaling by the measure of the domain would provide a better comparison against the L ∞ error which is not as large."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Example 3 (Rigid body rotation with diffusion, about ˆ z )","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":290.91454972278916,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":354.80027472,"t":299.65935824843757},"charspan":[0,58],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/35","text":"Example 3 (Rigid body rotation with diffusion, about ˆ z )"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"u t -yu x + xu y = d ( u xx + u yy + u zz ) + c ( x, y, z, t ) , x, y, z ∈ ( -2 π, 2 π ) (71)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":269.49454138100236,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":137.05500000000004,"r":523.27424062,"t":279.1244704484376},"charspan":[0,93],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/36","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"where the flow field describes rotation about the vector ˆ z . To test the accuracy of the scheme, we use the manufactured solution u ( x, y, z, t ) = exp( -( x 2 +2 y 2 +3 z 2 +3 dt )) with d = 1 / 3, for which the source term c ( x, y, z, t ) offsets the rotation and is","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":226.00290452567094,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":523.2774388600001,"t":258.6194704484376},"charspan":[0,272],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/37","text":"where the flow field describes rotation about the vector ˆ z . To test the accuracy of the scheme, we use the manufactured solution u ( x, y, z, t ) = exp( -( x 2 +2 y 2 +3 z 2 +3 dt )) with d = 1 / 3, for which the source term c ( x, y, z, t ) offsets the rotation and is"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"c ( x, y, z, t ) = e -( x 2 +2 y 2 +3 z 2 +3 dt ) ( -2 xy -d ( -9 + 4 x 2 +16 y 2 +36 z 2 ) ) . (72)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":202.5089045256709,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":143.0080000000001,"r":523.2755102800002,"t":215.86751864843757},"charspan":[0,100],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/38","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"As seen in Figure 5, the expected accuracies are observed for the RAIL scheme when using IMEX111, IMEX222 and IMEX443. We used a mesh size N = 80, tolerance ε = 10 -8 , final time T f = 0 . 3, and λ ranging from 0.5 to 2. When a low-rank source term is involved, we must express it in a Tucker tensor format. By inspection, it is straightforward for one to write down a Tucker decomposition of (72).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":143.70290452567087,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.00000000000017,"r":523.2751845800003,"t":188.27447044843757},"charspan":[0,399],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/39","text":"As seen in Figure 5, the expected accuracies are observed for the RAIL scheme when using IMEX111, IMEX222 and IMEX443. We used a mesh size N = 80, tolerance ε = 10 -8 , final time T f = 0 . 3, and λ ranging from 0.5 to 2. When a low-rank source term is involved, we must express it in a Tucker tensor format. By inspection, it is straightforward for one to write down a Tucker decomposition of (72)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"To test the rank of the solution, we set d = 1 / 12, c ( x, y, z, t ) = 0, double the speed of the rotation,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":131.7399045256709,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":86.94400000000023,"r":517.0416550000002,"t":140.44647044843748},"charspan":[0,108],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/40","text":"To test the rank of the solution, we set d = 1 / 12, c ( x, y, z, t ) = 0, double the speed of the rotation,"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"u t -2 yu x +2 xu y = 1 12 ( u xx + u yy + u zz ) , x, y, z ∈ ( -2 π, 2 π ) (73)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":99.70890452567096,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":157.05200000000025,"r":523.2721788200001,"t":121.98947044843749},"charspan":[0,80],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/41","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"and set the initial condition to u 0 ( x, y, z ) = exp( -( x 2 +9 y 2 + z 2 )). The solution rotates counterclockwise about the positive z -axis while slowly diffusing. Theoretically, the exact multilinear rank should be","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":70.09790452567097,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.00000000000006,"r":523.2776282,"t":92.33412004843751},"charspan":[0,220],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/42","text":"and set the initial condition to u 0 ( x, y, z ) = exp( -( x 2 +9 y 2 + z 2 )). The solution rotates counterclockwise about the positive z -axis while slowly diffusing. Theoretically, the exact multilinear rank should be"}],"version":"1.10.0"}